<h1>Canonicity</h1>

<p class="intro-description">Use this page to inspect how canonicity is configured, compare runs, replay calculations, and explicitly publish eligible rankings. Viewing and comparing do not change scores; only the publish action changes current display values.</p>

<section>
  <h2>Calculator identities</h2>
  <p class="section-description">These identifiers name the formula implementations used to calculate Philosopher and Work measures; they are not the formulae themselves. The versioned identity lets a later replay select exactly the same implementation. The calculators do not query or write to the database.</p>
  <table class="table table-striped">
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th>Philosopher</th>
        <td><code>philosopher-v2</code></td>
        <td><code>Canonicity::Philosopher::V2</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Work</th>
        <td><code>work-v2</code></td>
        <td><code>Canonicity::Work::V2</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Formulae</h2>
  <p class="section-description">These are the actual V2 equations. The normalized measure is used for analysis and replay; the display measure is the scaled value stored in a run snapshot. A zero normalization denominator produces a finite zero rather than NaN or infinity.</p>

  <h3>Normalization ideology</h3>
  <p><code>S₀ × S₁ × S₂ × … × Sₙ</code></p>
  <p>Every factor must be between 0 and 1 inclusive, so their product must also be between 0 and 1. Both calculators enforce this rule and raise an error identifying any component that is negative, non-finite, or greater than 1.0.</p>

  <h3>Scalar signals</h3>
  <p>A scalar signal is a single numeric value such as <code>mention</code> or <code>danker</code>. Its extrema are fixed for the run, and the value is divided by the relevant maximum to produce a factor in [0, 1]. Examples are <code>mention / max_mention</code> and <code>rank / max_rank</code>.</p>

  <h3>Bitset signals</h3>
  <p>A bitset signal is a set of on/off reference-source flags. Each flag has a weight in [0, 1], and the active contributions are summed, including <code>all_bonus</code> when it applies. The summed source-coverage factor must not exceed 1. Philosopher’s active bitset weights sum to exactly 1.00.</p>

  <h3>Derived components</h3>
  <p>Genre multipliers, role suppression, missing-value fallbacks, and linked-author adjustments are derivation rules applied to those raw signal types. They are not additional raw signal types, and their resulting factors must satisfy the same [0, 1] bound.</p>

  <h3>Philosopher V2</h3>
  <p><code>normalized_measure = (mention / max_mention) × (rank / max_rank) × total_source_weight</code></p>
  <p><code>display_measure = normalized_measure × 10,000,000</code></p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>mention</code>: effective mention count after missing-value and role-suppression rules.</li>
    <li><code>rank</code>: the entity’s Danker score, or the configured minimum-rank fallback when missing.</li>
    <li><code>max_mention</code> and <code>max_rank</code>: extrema from the dataset’s normalization population.</li>
    <li><code>total_source_weight</code>: active reference-source contributions plus the conditional <code>all_bonus</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Work V2</h3>
  <p><code>normalized_measure = (mention / max_mention) × (rank / max_rank) × genre × (sourcey + exists)</code></p>
  <p><code>display_measure = normalized_measure × 1,000,000</code></p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>mention</code>: the work’s mention count, with missing or zero counts defaulting to 0.5.</li>
    <li><code>rank</code>: the work’s Danker score, or the minimum-rank fallback when missing.</li>
    <li><code>genre</code>: the configured philosophical or non-philosophical genre multiplier.</li>
    <li><code>sourcey</code>: encyclopedia, conditional bonus, and PhilPapers contributions; zero coverage becomes 0.1.</li>
    <li><code>exists</code>: the linked-author adjustment, calculated before the <code>sourcey</code> fallback.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p class="section-description">Weights determine how much each source contributes to a measure. Active rows are available to the selected configuration. Inactive legacy rows remain visible for historical inspection; a <code>NULL</code> weight means the source has no current weight, rather than a numeric weight of zero.</p>
  <%= form_tag(canonicity_path, method: :get, class: "form-inline") do %>
    <%= label_tag :algorithm_version, "Algorithm version" %>
    <%= select_tag :algorithm_version, options_for_select(@algorithm_version_options, @algorithm_version), onchange: "this.form.submit()", style: "width: auto; max-width: 100%;" %>
    <%= submit_tag "Show", class: "slim" %>
  <% end %>

  <% if %w[2.0 2.1].include?(@algorithm_version) %>
    <% active_total = @weights.select(&:active?).sum { |weight| weight.weight_value || 0 } %>
    <p><strong>Philosopher total: <%= number_with_precision(active_total, precision: 6) %></strong> at stored precision.
    <% if @algorithm_version == "2.1" %>
      All active weights are stored directly at two decimal places and sum to exactly 1.00, including <code>all_bonus</code> at 0.08.
    <% else %>
      This is the original configuration restored for compatibility; its active weights sum to 1.51.
    <% end %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <table class="table table-striped table-hover">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Source</th>
        <th>Weight</th>
        <th>Status</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% @weights.each do |weight| %>
        <tr>
          <td><code><%= weight.source_name %></code></td>
          <td><%= weight.weight_value.nil? ? "NULL" : number_with_precision(weight.weight_value, precision: 2) %></td>
          <td>
            <% if weight.active? %>
              <span>Active</span>
            <% else %>
              <span style="color: #999;">Inactive legacy source</span>
            <% end %>
          </td>
          <td><%= weight.description %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Signals</h2>
  <p class="section-description">Signals are the raw inputs available to the calculators. “Changes over time” identifies values that may differ between imports or dataset captures. “Used in formula” distinguishes inputs that affect V2 measures from available inputs that are currently unused, such as <code>linkcount</code>.</p>
  <table class="table table-striped table-hover">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Description</th>
        <th>Source</th>
        <th>Changes over time</th>
        <th>Used in formula</th>
        <th>Status</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% if @signals.empty? %>
        <tr><td colspan="6">No signal definitions recorded for this configuration.</td></tr>
      <% end %>
      <% @signals.each do |signal| %>
        <tr>
          <td><code><%= signal.name %></code></td>
          <td><%= signal.description %></td>
          <td><%= signal.source %></td>
          <td><%= signal.subject_to_change? ? "Yes" : "No" %></td>
          <td><%= signal.used_in_formula? ? "Yes" : "No" %></td>
          <td><%= signal.active? ? "Active" : "Inactive" %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Input datasets</h2>
  <p class="section-description">A dataset is a frozen snapshot of the entities and primitive inputs used for calculation. Selected IDs receive run snapshots; normalization IDs determine the extrema. A ready dataset is complete, immutable, and consistent with the latest relevant source events; capturing is still in progress, and invalid identifies a failed or interrupted capture.</p>
  <table class="table table-striped table-hover">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>ID</th>
        <th>Subject</th>
        <th>Status</th>
        <th>Selected</th>
        <th>Normalization</th>
        <th>Philosopher run dependency</th>
        <th>Captured</th>
        <th>Failure</th>
        <th>Evidence</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% if @datasets.empty? %>
        <tr><td colspan="9">No input datasets have been captured yet.</td></tr>
      <% end %>
      <% @datasets.each do |dataset| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= dataset.id %></td>
          <td><%= dataset.subject_type %></td>
          <td><%= dataset.status %></td>
          <td><%= number_with_delimiter(dataset.selected_entity_ids.length) %></td>
          <td><%= number_with_delimiter(dataset.normalization_entity_ids.length) %></td>
          <td><%= dataset.input_run_id || "—" %></td>
          <td><%= dataset.capture_completed_at || "—" %></td>
          <td><%= dataset.failure_reason.presence || "—" %></td>
          <td>
            <details>
              <summary>View</summary>
              <strong>Normalization context</strong>
              <pre><%= JSON.pretty_generate(dataset.normalization_context) %></pre>
              <strong>Source provenance</strong>
              <pre><%= JSON.pretty_generate(dataset.source_provenance) %></pre>
            </details>
          </td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Source events</h2>
  <p class="section-description">Source events record what an import changed, including the artifact, entity, attribute, and typed old and new values. They are import evidence, not canonicity calculations. <code>NULL</code> preserves a missing value rather than coercing it to zero.</p>
  <table class="table table-striped table-hover">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Import</th>
        <th>Artifact</th>
        <th>Entity</th>
        <th>Attribute</th>
        <th>Old value</th>
        <th>New value</th>
        <th>Imported</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% if @source_events.empty? %>
        <tr><td colspan="7">No source imports have been recorded yet.</td></tr>
      <% end %>
      <% @source_events.each do |event| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= event.import_identity %></td>
          <td><%= event.artifact_identity %></td>
          <td><%= event.shadow_type %> Q<%= event.entity_id %></td>
          <td><code><%= event.attribute_name %></code></td>
          <td><%= event.old_value.nil? ? "NULL" : event.old_value %></td>
          <td><%= event.new_value.nil? ? "NULL" : event.new_value %></td>
          <td><%= event.imported_at %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Metric runs</h2>
  <p class="section-description">A run calculates measures from one immutable dataset using a recorded calculator and configuration. Versions are independent for Philosopher and Work. Recalculation reuses the same dataset, while replay reads a completed run and verifies its measures, population, normalization, and ranks without changing anything.</p>
  <table class="table table-striped table-hover">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>ID</th>
        <th>Subject</th>
        <th>Version</th>
        <th>Status</th>
        <th>Dataset</th>
        <th>Calculator</th>
        <th>Configuration</th>
        <th>Started</th>
        <th>Completed</th>
        <th>Failure</th>
        <th>Publication</th>
        <th></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% if @runs.empty? %>
        <tr><td colspan="12">No metric runs have been calculated yet.</td></tr>
      <% end %>
      <% @runs.each do |run| %>
        <% eligibility = @publication_eligibilities.fetch(run.id) %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= run.id %></td>
          <td><%= run.subject_type %></td>
          <td><%= run.version %></td>
          <td><%= run.status %></td>
          <td><%= run.metric_input_dataset_id %></td>
          <td><code><%= run.calculator_identity %></code></td>
          <td><%= run.configuration_version %></td>
          <td><%= run.started_at %></td>
          <td><%= run.completed_at || "—" %></td>
          <td><%= run.failure_reason.presence || "—" %></td>
          <td>
            <% if eligibility.eligible? %>
              <span>Eligible</span>
            <% else %>
              <details>
                <summary>Not eligible</summary>
                <ul><% eligibility.reasons.each do |reason| %><li><%= reason %></li><% end %></ul>
              </details>
            <% end %>
          </td>
          <td><%= link_to "Show", canonicity_run_path(run) %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Publication history</h2>
  <p class="section-description">Publication explicitly promotes an eligible full-population run to the measures and dense ranks shown elsewhere on the site. History is append-only: republishing an older run creates a new record, and the highest publication ID for each subject is current.</p>
  <table class="table table-striped table-hover">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>ID</th><th>Subject</th><th>Run</th><th>Published</th><th>Status</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% if @publications.empty? %>
        <tr><td colspan="5">No runs have been published yet.</td></tr>
      <% end %>
      <% @publications.each do |publication| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= publication.id %></td>
          <td><%= publication.subject_type %></td>
          <td><%= link_to publication.metric_run.version, canonicity_run_path(publication.metric_run) %></td>
          <td><%= publication.created_at %></td>
          <td><%= @current_publications[publication.subject_type] == publication ? "Current" : "Historical" %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Run comparison</h2>
  <p class="section-description">Compare two runs of the same subject to see measure and dense-rank movement, added and missing entities, population and normalization changes, and calculator or configuration differences. Comparison is read-only.</p>
  <%= form_tag(canonicity_path, method: :get, class: "form-inline") do %>
    <% options = @runs.map { |run| ["#{run.subject_type} #{run.version} (ID #{run.id})", run.id] } %>
    <%= label_tag :from_run_id, "From run" %>
    <%= select_tag :from_run_id, options_for_select(options, params[:from_run_id]), include_blank: true %>
    <%= label_tag :to_run_id, "To run" %>
    <%= select_tag :to_run_id, options_for_select(options, params[:to_run_id]), include_blank: true %>
    <%= submit_tag "Compare", class: "slim" %>
  <% end %>
  <% if @comparison_error %>
    <p><strong><%= @comparison_error %></strong></p>
  <% elsif @comparison %>
    <% report = @comparison.to_h %>
    <p>
      <%= link_to "#{report[:from_run][:subject_type]} #{report[:from_run][:version]}", canonicity_run_path(report[:from_run][:id]) %>
      →
      <%= link_to "#{report[:to_run][:subject_type]} #{report[:to_run][:version]}", canonicity_run_path(report[:to_run][:id]) %>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Added entities: <%= @comparison.added_entities.length %> (<%= @comparison.added_entities.join(", ").presence || "none" %>)</li>
      <li>Missing entities: <%= @comparison.missing_entities.length %> (<%= @comparison.missing_entities.join(", ").presence || "none" %>)</li>
      <li>Changed entities: <%= @comparison.changes.length %>; unchanged entities: <%= @comparison.unchanged_entity_count %></li>
      <li>Calculator: <code><%= report[:calculator][:from] %></code> → <code><%= report[:calculator][:to] %></code></li>
      <li>Configuration: <%= report[:configuration][:from_version] %> → <%= report[:configuration][:to_version] %>; fingerprint changed: <%= report[:configuration][:changed] %></li>
    </ul>
    <details>
      <summary>Population and normalization differences</summary>
      <pre><%= JSON.pretty_generate(report[:population]) %></pre>
    </details>
    <table class="table table-striped table-hover">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Shadow</th><th>Entity</th><th>From measure</th><th>To measure</th><th>Change</th><th>From rank</th><th>To rank</th><th>Rank change</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% if @comparison.changes.empty? %>
          <tr><td colspan="8">No measure or rank changes.</td></tr>
        <% end %>
        <% @comparison.changes.first(100).each do |change| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= change[:shadow_id] %></td>
            <td><%= change[:label] || "Q#{change[:entity_id]}" %></td>
            <td><%= change[:from_measure] %></td>
            <td><%= change[:to_measure] %></td>
            <td><%= change[:measure_change] %></td>
            <td><%= change[:from_rank] %></td>
            <td><%= change[:to_rank] %></td>
            <td><%= change[:rank_change] %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <% if @comparison.changes.length > 100 %>
      <p>Showing the first 100 changed entities. Use <code>metric:compare</code> for the complete report.</p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Metric infrastructure</h2>
  <p class="section-description">These counts show how much execution evidence exists. Datasets freeze calculator inputs; runs calculate measures from a dataset; source events record imports; publications record promotion to current rankings. Zero counts are expected before those operations begin.</p>
  <table class="table table-striped">
    <tbody>
      <% @infrastructure_counts.each do |label, count| %>
        <tr>
          <th><%= label.to_s.humanize %></th>
          <td><%= number_with_delimiter(count) %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<p><%= link_to "Reference works", dictionaries_path %></p>
